1851
02:34:46,256 --> 02:34:52,868
So imagine you are a smart alien and you have access to all the materials that are published.

1852
02:34:53,166 --> 02:35:02,176
For example, let's say books or blogs or any material that is publicly available, and you also know the mathematics

1853
02:35:02,336 --> 02:35:03,776
and you also can think.

1854
02:35:04,496 --> 02:35:08,256
But imagine you don't know anything about the language, right?

1855
02:35:08,590 --> 02:35:10,596
So now here is your task.

1856
02:35:10,926 --> 02:35:17,604
Assume I gave you an incomplete sentence and your job is to predict the next word.

1857
02:35:18,254 --> 02:35:23,296
The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy blank, right?

1858
02:35:23,598 --> 02:35:27,776
So you have to predict the next word. Remember, you don't know the language.

1859
02:35:28,014 --> 02:35:30,788
So how do you think you can go about solving this issue?

1860
02:35:31,470 --> 02:35:39,776
So some possible ways could be going through all the available books and trying to search if we can actually find this sentence

1861
02:35:40,174 --> 02:35:41,936
in those books, right?

1862
02:35:42,254 --> 02:35:53,136
So assume this sentence is present 100 times in the available books. Then we probably would choose the highest probability word that comes next, right?

1863
02:35:53,326 --> 02:35:59,748
That is the word which comes more frequently, right? So that will be your next prediction word.

1864
02:35:59,936 --> 02:36:07,616
So that's how we may be able to predict the next word, but how will we predict the next to next words, right?

1865
02:36:07,776 --> 02:36:12,964
So that's where the problem arises in predicting the next to next words.

1866
02:36:13,376 --> 02:36:18,656
And assume in English we have 40,000 vocabulary words.

1867
02:36:18,816 --> 02:36:19,136
Okay.

1868
02:36:19,536 --> 02:36:26,096
So to predict that one next word, we will have 40,000 options, right?

1869
02:36:26,656 --> 02:36:34,884
And to predict the next two next word, that is two words, we will have 40,000 times 40,000 options, right?

1870
02:36:35,246 --> 02:36:39,812
And to predict the next 20 words, 40,000 times 20.

1871
02:36:40,016 --> 02:36:44,228
This is more options than the number of particles that we have in the universe.

1872
02:36:44,416 --> 02:36:49,216
So you can imagine, right? So this may become an impossible problem to solve.

1873
02:36:49,536 --> 02:36:57,776
So what if the given sentence doesn't even come anywhere in the past data, be it books or any materials that is publicly available,

1874
02:36:58,446 --> 02:37:02,336
There is no way we can predict the next words in that case, right?

1875
02:37:02,606 --> 02:37:05,156
And let me show one another example.

1876
02:37:05,678 --> 02:37:08,896
So this is the learning tower paisa experiment, right?

1877
02:37:09,456 --> 02:37:17,776
So assume you are a scientist and you drop a material, so in this case an apple, from the top of the tower

1878
02:37:17,936 --> 02:37:30,816
and you want to calculate the time it takes to reach the ground, right? So once we collect this data after performing several experiments, what do you think we will do once we have the data collected?

1879
02:37:31,118 --> 02:37:44,896
We will try to find the patterns in the data, right? So that means we will try to create a model for how much time the material took to reach the ground from the top of the tower.

1880
02:37:45,136 --> 02:37:51,300
So the equation is in this case t is the time, h is the height, g is the gravity.

1881
02:37:51,696 --> 02:37:54,496
So this is how we came up with this equation.

1882
02:37:54,734 --> 02:37:56,416
So what will this model do?

1883
02:37:56,576 --> 02:38:03,856
Once we send the input to this model, then the model can get the output as the result for us.

1884
02:38:04,256 --> 02:38:10,016
So similarly, let's go back to our next prediction from a given sentence example.

1885
02:38:10,176 --> 02:38:19,108
Right. So what if we send the given set of words as an input to a model in order to predict the next token,

1886
02:38:19,456 --> 02:38:29,616
right? So a model which takes a sequence of words as an input and predicts the next token is called a language model.

1887
02:38:30,318 --> 02:38:37,252
So, with this, I hope you understood what a language model is, and we will deep dive in the next lecture.

1888
02:38:37,618 --> 02:38:38,864
All right, Thank you.

1889
02:38:39,104 --> 02:38:48,644
So, we understood in the previous lecture that the language model is nothing but a model which takes a sequence of words as an input and predicts the

1890
02:38:49,038 --> 02:38:58,756
next token that is called a language model. Now, let's try to understand why the model is called as a large language model.

1891
02:38:59,638 --> 02:38:59,698
Okay.

1892
02:39:00,064 --> 02:39:11,076
For example, let's take these graphs, right. So, if we take y is equal to a x plus b, how many parameters do we have here? We have two parameters a and b.

1893
02:39:11,218 --> 02:39:20,932
It's a simple straight line, right. Now, let's see the quadratic graph, right. So, we have three parameters here a, b and c.

1894
02:39:21,262 --> 02:39:29,984
And the next graph is we have four parameters here a, b, c, and d. Now, let me tell you the facts about LLMs.

1895
02:39:30,224 --> 02:39:39,958
LLMs have billions of parameters, and if we see the scale of this, we can easily visualize a two-dimensional surface and three-dimensional

1896
02:39:40,144 --> 02:39:44,384
surface as well. And it's hard to visualize a four-dimensional surface, right?

1897
02:39:44,624 --> 02:39:49,984
Then imagine how can you visualize a model which has billions of parameters.

1898
02:39:50,338 --> 02:40:01,424
It's hard to analyze with our brains, right? Also, each parameter has a certain value which is optimized so that we can predict the next token.

1899
02:40:01,584 --> 02:40:05,252
So, that's why these models are called large language models.

1900
02:40:05,424 --> 02:40:14,784
And these LLMs or large language models help us to predict the next token given a set of words as an input sequence,

1901
02:40:15,584 --> 02:40:27,184
So, if the model is predicting the next token, why are these models called language models? Maybe they should be called next token prediction models, right? So we will come to that point.

