4235
06:12:49,518 --> 06:12:52,964
This PDF is about how organizations are rewiring to capture value.

4236
06:12:53,328 --> 06:12:59,012
And this PDF has a lot of text, charts, and how Gen AI is used in the organization.

4237
06:12:59,248 --> 06:13:04,228
Also, this PDF was prepared by McKinsey, but you can use any document you want.

4238
06:13:04,526 --> 06:13:11,076
So we have this PDF as our input document, and then we ask a question to LLM as who is Bryce Hall.

4239
06:13:11,406 --> 06:13:16,612
You can see if you search by doing control F, Bryce Hall is mentioned three times within the PDF.

4240
06:13:17,454 --> 06:13:21,860
First, let's understand what happens once we ask the question to the LLM.

4241
06:13:22,542 --> 06:13:29,828
The LLM loads this entire PDF document into the LLM's context window and then the LLM will try to answer.

4242
06:13:30,382 --> 06:13:33,124
This approach works better when we have a few documents.

4243
06:13:33,518 --> 06:13:41,316
But when we have several PDFs, for example, hundreds of PDFs, then loading all these documents into the context window can be problematic, right?

4244
06:13:41,614 --> 06:13:45,284
Because we know each LLM has a limited context window size.

4245
06:13:45,518 --> 06:13:52,676
As we feed more documents to the LLM, then the LLM may start losing the context and it can start hallucinating the results.

4246
06:13:52,928 --> 06:13:56,644
Just like humans, we cannot retain a lot of information, right?

4247
06:13:57,008 --> 06:14:02,884
And to resolve this issue, they introduced RAG retrieval augmented generation.

4248
06:14:03,214 --> 06:14:09,188
The concept of RAG is when we send a prompt or input sequence that is who is Bryce Hall to the LLM.

4249
06:14:10,190 --> 06:14:13,284
In our example, our PDF has 26 pages, right?

4250
06:14:14,830 --> 06:14:22,884
So we convert the text on each page into higher dimensional vectors. Let's assume we convert each page into 300 dimensional vectors.

4251
06:14:23,278 --> 06:14:27,460
So we have 300 dimensional vectors for each of these 26 pages.

4252
06:14:27,886 --> 06:14:31,392
And what do we mean by converting into 300 dimensional vectors?

4253
06:14:31,632 --> 06:14:33,952
We have a concept called word embedding.

4254
06:14:34,158 --> 06:14:38,468
By using this, we can represent text into higher dimensional vectors.

4255
06:14:39,438 --> 06:14:47,952
So each one in green contains compressed information of the entire text present on each page in red, starting from page 1, 2,

4256
06:14:48,192 --> 06:14:50,832
3, and so on till page 26.

4257
06:14:51,118 --> 06:14:54,340
So each vector represents the meaning of the text present in each page.

4258
06:14:54,702 --> 06:14:56,192
And how are they converted?

4259
06:14:56,846 --> 06:15:02,752
We have some amazing techniques like word to vec. We can use these and meaning can be captured into vectors.

4260
06:15:02,832 --> 06:15:03,152
Okay.

4261
06:15:03,472 --> 06:15:08,356
So the intent of word embedding is capturing the text into a vector effectively.

4262
06:15:09,102 --> 06:15:16,112
And then we will convert our prompt or the input sequence that we will be providing to the LLM into vectors as well.

4263
06:15:16,302 --> 06:15:20,772
After that, we will see which one of these 26 vectors is closer to the prompt vector.

4264
06:15:21,294 --> 06:15:24,516
Closer means either cosine similarity or Euclidean distance.

4265
06:15:24,814 --> 06:15:28,272
By using this, we can identify if two vectors are similar to each other.

4266
06:15:28,526 --> 06:15:32,708
That is, if the dot product is higher, then those two vectors are similar to each other.

4267
06:15:33,006 --> 06:15:43,952
Once we find the dot product between the prompt and each one of the 26 vectors, then we will retrieve the top three pages or documents that have the highest similarity with the prompt.

4268
06:15:44,206 --> 06:15:47,748
Assume the yellow highlighted ones have the highest similarity with our prompt.

4269
06:15:48,112 --> 06:15:55,652
Then instead of the entire PDF with 26 pages, we will only be passing the top 3 pages that have the highest similarity.

4270
06:15:55,950 --> 06:16:03,940
In our example, it will be page 2, 3 and 26 along with our prompt to the LLM in order to retrieve the answer.

4271
06:16:04,494 --> 06:16:10,372
So along with our prompt, we have passed only the information which is relevant to the prompt to the LLM.

4272
06:16:10,592 --> 06:16:18,660
That is only the retrieved documents or pages page 2, 3 and 26 to the LLM along with our prompt.

4273
06:16:18,894 --> 06:16:25,792
That is, instead of passing the entire PDF with 26 pages, we passed only pages 2, 3 and 26.

4274
06:16:25,966 --> 06:16:30,820
These are the retrieved documents that are very similar to our input sequence or prompt.

4275
06:16:30,992 --> 06:16:31,312
Okay.

4276
06:16:31,886 --> 06:16:34,912
So you can see the difference between these two scenarios, right?

4277
06:16:35,072 --> 06:16:43,844
In the first case, everything has been passed to the LLM, whereas in the second case, only the relevant retrieved documents have been passed to the LLM.

4278
06:16:44,174 --> 06:16:52,432
That's why they named it Retrieval Augmented Generation, because we are taking the retrieved document that is pages 2, 3, and 26,

4279
06:16:52,752 --> 06:16:56,832
and then we are augmenting with our prompt and then generating the answer.

4280
06:16:57,166 --> 06:17:00,272
That's why the name Retrieval Augmented Generation R A G.

4281
06:17:00,752 --> 06:17:11,952
Sometimes we call this grounded generation as well, because we are passing the most relevant information only the pages 2, 3, and 26. So the answer will be more grounded in reality, right?

4282
06:17:12,206 --> 06:17:21,792
The biggest advantage of using RAG is that LLM's hallucinations can be reduced because we are passing the most relevant information to the LLM along with our input prompt.

4283
06:17:22,032 --> 06:17:25,380
This is the whole concept of RAG retrieval augmented generation.

4284
06:17:26,382 --> 06:17:28,548
Let me explain RAG in another way.

4285
06:17:28,912 --> 06:17:33,952
Understanding these concepts are very important before we implement the hands-on project. Okay.

